*Fault catalog.* Every part can fail in each of the modes of its kind (@tab:faults), and each simulated unit draws the severity of its fault (a short's residual resistance, how far a value has drifted), so a catalogued fault is a distribution, not one number. Units outside the catalogue test "no single fault fits": most carry two faults on different parts, drawn from faults that usually cause a symptom; the rest carry a solder bridge between two nodes of one stage, or a passive part fitted with a wrong value.

#figure(
  table(
    columns: (1.0fr, 3.2fr),
    table.header([Part kind], [Failure modes (severity drawn per unit)]),
    [Resistor], [Open; short (below an ohm); value drift to a half, double or ten times],
    [Film capacitor], [Open; short],
    [Electrolytic capacitor], [Open; short; capacitance down to a half or a tenth; series resistance up ten- to a hundredfold],
    [Potentiometer], [Wiper open; track open on either side],
    [Diode, zener], [Open; short],
    [Transistor], [Collector-emitter short; base open; gain down to a fifth; collector-base leakage],
    [Triode], [Low emission; heater open],
    [Op-amp], [Output stuck at a rail; gain-bandwidth down ten- to a hundredfold],
  ),
  caption: [Fault modes by part kind (<< m('library.fault_mode_count') >> in all). Every mode of every part is one hypothesis; with "healthy" and "no single fault fits" the channel strip has << lib.channel_strip.hypotheses + 1 >> hypotheses.],
) <tab:faults>

*Monte Carlo design.* A simulated unit draws every part within its tolerance: values from a normal distribution with a standard deviation of half the tolerance, truncated at the tolerance; electrolytic series resistance, transistor gain, tube amplification and emission, op-amp bandwidth and the mains level from their own spreads. The stress sets widen every spread << design.simulation.stress_tolerance_scale >> times. Each hypothesis gets << design.simulation.train_draws >> training and << design.simulation.val_draws >> validation units. Every unit's random numbers derive from a seed built from the base seed, circuit, split, hypothesis and draw, so any unit can be regenerated alone; a test re-simulates stored units and compares them to six significant figures. Units are simulated in ngspice batches of << design.simulation.chunk_draws >>, with a three-step retry ladder (finer convergence settings, then a stiffer integration method) for batches that fail; failed units are kept with a reason and excluded from training. Of << num(m('library.simulations.total_draws'), 0) >> units, << m('library.simulations.failed_draws') >> failed and << m('library.simulations.retried_draws') >> needed a retry. A manifest records the hashes of every netlist, the device library and the tolerance code, and the simulator version. Each unit is simulated at its DC operating point, for gain at 20 Hz, 1 kHz and 20 kHz, for hum at 120 Hz, and for distortion at 1 kHz from a transient and Fourier analysis.

*Measurement model.* A simulated reading is the true value plus instrument error: a meter of ±(<< num(design.measurement.dmm_pct, 1) >>% + << design.measurement.dmm_counts >> counts) on 6,000-count ranges, rounded to the range's resolution; an oscilloscope of ±<< num(design.measurement.scope_pct, 0) >>%, with floors for tiny signals; a distortion reading of ±<< num(design.measurement.thd_pct, 0) >>%. An unsoldered part tested out of circuit returns only "within" or "out of tolerance", with sensitivity << design.measurement.lift_sensitivity >> and << num(design.measurement.lift_false_positive_pct, 0) >>% false positives. The engine works in transformed units (DC voltages on a scale that is linear near zero and logarithmic above it, gains and hum in decibels), where instrument error is close to constant.

*Data splits.* @tab:splits lists every set. Evaluation sets sample symptomatic units uniformly: each case draws a hypothesis and a unit and keeps it only if the unit shows a symptom (up to << design.cases.max_attempts >> draws), so faults appear in proportion to how often they reach a bench; faults that never showed a symptom in training are left out.

