#decision("Simulate every fault under tolerances instead of one nominal fault dictionary")[
*Options:* the classic fault dictionary (one nominal simulation per fault, then nearest match) or many simulated units per fault with every part inside its tolerance. *Choice:* many units per fault. *Why:* tolerance spread is exactly what makes bench diagnosis hard, and probabilities need the spread of each fault's readings, not one point. *Consequence:* << num(m('library.simulations.total_draws'), 0) >> simulations and learned likelihoods, and ambiguity groups measured from data rather than assumed.
]

<< part(3, "Diagnosis engine and evaluation plan") >>
<% set g = m('library.groups.channel_strip') %>

*Likelihoods.* The engine needs the probability of any subset of readings under every hypothesis, because the technician measures one thing at a time. The core model fits, for each hypothesis, a Gaussian mixture of one to << design.engine.gmm_max_components >> full-covariance components on its training units in transformed units, choosing the number by the Bayesian information criterion (k components only with at least << design.engine.gmm_min_samples_per_component >>k units), and adds instrument noise to each component. Any subset of readings is then handled exactly, by keeping only the observed dimensions. The alternative, a LightGBM classifier, is trained on << design.engine.disc_masked_copies >> copies of the training units with readings hidden at random (each row keeps each reading with a probability drawn between << design.engine.disc_keep_p[0] >> and << design.engine.disc_keep_p[1] >>) and temperature-scaled on masked validation units; its posterior divided by the training class frequencies is a likelihood up to a constant, so both models feed the same update and the same measurement choice.

*"No single fault fits".* A further hypothesis with prior << design.engine.unmodeled_prior >> gives every reading a flat density over its plausible range. Its level per observed reading is calibrated on validation units: the largest level that flags at most << pct(design.calibration.false_alarm_budget, 0) >> of symptomatic single faults (<< design.calibration.single_units.channel_strip >> channel-strip units, << design.calibration.single_units.driver >> per block) against double-fault and modified units. It is calibrated twice, for sessions whose prior was read from a complaint and for all others, because a complaint concentrates the prior and changes what "no fit" competes with.

*Ambiguity groups.* Some faults give the same readings at every test point. Each hypothesis's << design.engine.group_draws_per_fault >> validation units are scored with every reading; hypotheses confused with each other at a mean rate of at least << design.engine.group_threshold >> are joined, and groups are the connected sets. On the channel strip << g.hypotheses >> hypotheses form << g.groups >> groups, << g.singletons >> of size one and none larger than << g.max_size >>. The engine reports groups, and accuracy is scored on them.

*Measurement choice and stopping.* For every affordable measurement the engine estimates the expected information gain over the group posterior and "no single fault fits", from << design.engine.eig_samples >> readings sampled from the current predictive mixture, and divides it by the measurement's effort; for a lift test the two outcomes are enumerated exactly. Near-ties go to the low-voltage, then the cheaper, measurement. A session stops when one group or "no single fault fits" reaches << pct(design.engine.stop_threshold, 0) >>, when the budget of << num(design.engine.budget, 0) >> effort units cannot pay for another measurement, or when no measurement promises << design.engine.eig_min_bits >> bit.

*Complaint prior.* << design.symptoms.n_features >> symptom features (no output, low gain, hum, distortion, DC at the output, bias drift, bass or treble loss, a dead or drifted rail) are computed from every simulated unit with fixed thresholds, for example gain more than << num(-design.symptoms.low_gain_db, 0) >> dB low or hum << num(design.symptoms.hum_margin_db, 0) >> dB above the healthy spread. How often each fault shows each feature gives a likelihood for the features a complaint reports; two smoothing parameters are chosen on validation complaints. A complaint that reports nothing recognised leaves the prior uniform.

*Evaluation plan.* Every system runs on the same test units with the same instrument noise (common random numbers), so comparisons are paired (@tab:systems). Statistics: accuracies and paired differences get percentile bootstrap intervals (<< num(design.statistics.bootstrap_resamples, 0) >> resamples), effort ratios a bootstrap of the ratio of means on the same units, paired accuracy an exact McNemar test, calibration a << design.statistics.ece_bins >>-bin expected calibration error over every step with a bootstrap over units (<< design.statistics.step_ece_case_resamples >> resamples), "no single fault fits" an AUROC with a bootstrap, and zero-event targets an exact binomial bound. Bars come from the pilot by the rule fixed in M1: the pilot estimate moved << design.statistics.bar_k >> standard errors against the system, pooled with the test set's circuit mix (channel strip << design.statistics.bar_weights.channel_strip >>, each block << design.statistics.bar_weights.driver >>). The failure analysis breaks misses down by circuit, part kind, fault mode, symptom rate and group size, and separates wrong names from "no single fault fits" calls.

